          `CPU, yaani **Central Processing Unit**, computer ka dimaag hai — har calculation, har faisla aur har instruction yahin se guzarta hai. CPU ke andar teen main hisse hote hain. **ALU (Arithmetic Logic Unit)** jod, ghata, guna aur compare jaise kaam karta hai. **Control Unit (CU)** yeh tay karta hai ki kaunsi instruction kab chalegi aur data kahan se kahan jaayega. Aur **registers** CPU ke andar ki bahut chhoti, bahut tez memory hoti hai jismein woh values rakhi jaati hain jin par abhi kaam chal raha hai.

Sabse important registers ko yaad rakho: **PC (Program Counter)** agli instruction ka address rakhta hai, **IR (Instruction Register)** woh instruction rakhta hai jo abhi chal rahi hai, aur **ACC (Accumulator)** calculation ka result rakhta hai. Iske alawa **MAR** aur **MDR** memory se baat karne ke liye hote hain, aur general-purpose registers jaise AX, BX values ko pakad kar rakhte hain. Register RAM se hazaron guna tez hota hai, isliye CPU pehle data ko register mein laata hai, phir us par kaam karta hai.

CPU akele kaam nahi karta — woh **RAM** se instructions aur data leta hai, unhe process karta hai aur result wapas bhejta hai. Yeh poora kaam ek fixed rhythm mein hota hai jise **clock** kehte hain: har clock tick par CPU ek chhota sa step karta hai. Clock speed **GHz** mein napi jaati hai — 3 GHz matlab ek second mein 3 arab ticks. Par zyada GHz ka matlab hamesha zyada speed nahi hota, kyunki ek instruction ko poora karne mein kitne cycles lagte hain, yeh bhi matter karta hai.`,
          `The CPU, or **Central Processing Unit**, is the brain of the computer — every calculation, every decision, and every instruction passes through it. The CPU has three main parts inside it. The **ALU (Arithmetic Logic Unit)** does addition, subtraction, multiplication, and comparison. The **Control Unit (CU)** decides which instruction runs when, and where data moves. And **registers** are very small, very fast memory inside the CPU that holds the values being worked on right now.

Remember the most important registers: the **PC (Program Counter)** holds the address of the next instruction, the **IR (Instruction Register)** holds the instruction running right now, and the **ACC (Accumulator)** holds the result of a calculation. In addition, **MAR** and **MDR** talk to memory, and general-purpose registers like AX and BX hold values. A register is thousands of times faster than RAM, so the CPU first brings data into a register and then works on it.

The CPU does not work alone — it takes instructions and data from **RAM**, processes them, and sends the result back. All of this work happens in a fixed rhythm called the **clock**: on every clock tick the CPU takes one small step. Clock speed is measured in **GHz** — 3 GHz means 3 billion ticks per second. But a higher GHz does not always mean higher speed, because how many cycles one instruction takes also matters.`
