          `CPU ke baare mein sabse badi galti hai yeh sochna ki **zyada GHz matlab hamesha tez computer**. Ek 3 GHz ka purana CPU ek 2.5 GHz ke naye CPU se slow ho sakta hai, kyunki naya CPU har cycle mein zyada kaam kar deta hai. Asli formula hai: time = (instructions x CPI) / clock speed. **CPI** matlab cycles per instruction — agar ek CPU ka CPI 2.5 hai aur doosre ka 1.5, toh doosra kam GHz par bhi tez hoga. Isi liye aaj ke CPUs mein cores, cache aur pipeline par zyada dhyaan diya jaata hai, sirf GHz badhane par nahi.

**Galti 2: Register aur RAM ko ek samajhna.** Beginners aksar kehte hain "CPU ke paas memory hoti hai" — haan, par woh RAM nahi, registers hote hain. Register CPU ke andar hota hai, uski size sirf kuch bytes hoti hai aur woh nanosecond mein data deta hai. RAM CPU ke bahar hoti hai, GBs mein hoti hai aur data dene mein 100 guna zyada time leti hai. Isi gap ko bharne ke liye **cache** hota hai — L1, L2, L3 cache registers aur RAM ke beech ki tez memory hai. Agar aapka program baar-baar ek hi data ko use karta hai, toh woh cache mein rehta hai aur program tez chalta hai.

**Galti 3: Har instruction ko 1 cycle samajhna.** Simple instructions jaise \`ADD\` 1 cycle mein ho sakti hain, par memory se data laane wali instruction mein kai cycles lag sakte hain, kyunki RAM slow hai. Isi ko **memory wall** kehte hain — CPU tez hai, par memory uska saath nahi de paati. Pipelining aur cache isi problem ke solutions hain.`,
          `The biggest mistake about the CPU is thinking that **more GHz always means a faster computer**. A 3 GHz old CPU can be slower than a 2.5 GHz new CPU, because the new CPU does more work in every cycle. The real formula is: time = (instructions x CPI) / clock speed. **CPI** means cycles per instruction — if one CPU has a CPI of 2.5 and another has 1.5, the second one is faster even at a lower GHz. That is why modern CPUs focus on cores, cache, and pipelining, not just on raising GHz.

**Mistake 2: Confusing registers with RAM.** Beginners often say "the CPU has memory" — yes, but that is registers, not RAM. A register sits inside the CPU, holds only a few bytes, and gives data in nanoseconds. RAM sits outside the CPU, holds gigabytes, and takes about 100 times longer to give data. **Cache** fills this gap — L1, L2, and L3 caches are fast memory between the registers and RAM. If your program uses the same data again and again, it stays in the cache and the program runs fast.

**Mistake 3: Assuming every instruction takes 1 cycle.** A simple instruction like \`ADD\` can finish in 1 cycle, but an instruction that fetches data from memory can take many cycles, because RAM is slow. This is called the **memory wall** — the CPU is fast, but memory cannot keep up with it. Pipelining and cache are the fixes for this problem.`
