          `Chalo poora article ek jagah bandhte hain. **CPU** ke teen hisse hain: **ALU** calculation karta hai, **Control Unit** instructions ko chalata hai, aur **registers** (PC, IR, ACC) tez temporary storage hote hain. Har instruction **fetch-execute cycle** se guzarti hai: PC batata hai kahan se laani hai, IR mein aati hai, CU decode karta hai, ALU execute karta hai, aur phir PC aage badhta hai. Hamare dry run mein \`LOAD 5, ADD 3, SUB 1\` ka final ACC 7 bana.

**Clock speed** (GHz) sirf ek hissa hai — asli speed (instructions x CPI) / clock speed se nikalti hai, aur **CPI** jitna kam, utna behtar. **Pipelining** 5 stages (Fetch, Decode, Execute, Memory, Write-back) ko overlap karke throughput badhati hai: 10 instructions par 50 ki jagah 14 cycles. **Cache** RAM aur CPU ke beech ke gap ko bharta hai, aur **multi-core** sach mein parallel kaam deta hai. Hazards — data, control aur structural — pipeline ko rok sakte hain, aur branch prediction galat hone par pipeline flush hoti hai.

Interview mein CPU ke sawaal aksar aise aate hain: "fetch-execute cycle samjhao", "CPI kya hai", "pipelining se kya fayda hai", "cache kyun zaroori hai". In sab ka answer ek hi line mein hai: CPU har instruction ko fetch-decode-execute karta hai, aur tez hone ke liye woh ek saath zyada kaam (pipeline, cache, cores) karta hai, ek kaam ko tez karke nahi. Agar aap dry run bina dekhe trace kar lete ho, toh CPU aapko aa gaya hai. Agle article mein hum kernel dekhenge — woh software jo is CPU ko chalata hai.`,
          `Let us tie the whole article together. The **CPU** has three parts: the **ALU** calculates, the **Control Unit** runs instructions, and **registers** (PC, IR, ACC) are fast temporary storage. Every instruction goes through the **fetch-execute cycle**: the PC says where to fetch it from, it arrives in the IR, the CU decodes it, the ALU executes it, and then the PC moves ahead. In our dry run, \`LOAD 5, ADD 3, SUB 1\` gave a final ACC of 7.

**Clock speed** (GHz) is only one part — real speed comes from (instructions x CPI) / clock speed, and a lower **CPI** is better. **Pipelining** overlaps 5 stages (Fetch, Decode, Execute, Memory, Write-back) to raise throughput: 14 cycles instead of 50 for 10 instructions. **Cache** fills the gap between RAM and the CPU, and **multi-core** gives true parallel work. Hazards — data, control, and structural — can stall the pipeline, and a wrong branch prediction flushes it.

Interview questions on the CPU often sound like this: "explain the fetch-execute cycle", "what is CPI", "what is the benefit of pipelining", "why is cache needed". The answer to all of them is one line: the CPU fetches, decodes, and executes every instruction, and it gets faster by doing more work at once (pipeline, cache, cores), not by making one job faster. If you can trace the dry run without looking, you know the CPU. In the next article we will look at the kernel — the software that drives this CPU.`
