          `**Kernel** operating system ka sabse core hissa hai — woh program jo computer on hote hi load hota hai aur band hone tak memory mein rehta hai. Jab aap koi app kholte ho, file save karte ho ya Wi-Fi connect karte ho, toh aap seedha hardware se baat nahi karte — aap kernel se request karte ho, aur kernel hardware se baat karta hai. Kernel ke main kaam hain: **process management** (kaunsi process kab chalegi), **memory management** (kis ko kitni memory milegi), **device management** (disk, keyboard, printer) aur **system calls** ko handle karna.

Kernel ko ek **hotel ka manager** samjho. Guests (user programs) seedha kitchen (hardware) mein nahi jaate — woh manager ko order dete hain, manager kitchen se kaam karwata hai aur result wapas deta hai. Agar har guest seedha kitchen mein ghus jaaye toh gadbad ho jaayegi — koi gas band kar dega, koi doosre ka khana kha lega. Isi liye kernel beech mein hai: woh rules lagata hai aur hardware ko protect karta hai.

Ek important baat: **operating system aur kernel ek nahi hain**. Kernel OS ka core hai, aur OS mein kernel ke saath utilities, shell, libraries aur apps bhi aate hain. Linux ek kernel hai, jabki Ubuntu ek poora operating system hai jo Linux kernel par bana hai. Interview mein yeh farak aksar poochha jaata hai, isliye yaad rakho.`,
          `The **kernel** is the innermost part of the operating system — the program that loads when the computer starts and stays in memory until shutdown. When you open an app, save a file, or connect to Wi-Fi, you do not talk to the hardware directly — you send a request to the kernel, and the kernel talks to the hardware. The main jobs of the kernel are: **process management** (which process runs when), **memory management** (who gets how much memory), **device management** (disk, keyboard, printer), and handling **system calls**.

Think of the kernel as a **hotel manager**. Guests (user programs) do not walk straight into the kitchen (the hardware) — they give their order to the manager, the manager gets the work done in the kitchen, and brings back the result. If every guest walked into the kitchen, there would be chaos — someone would switch off the gas, someone would eat another guest's food. That is why the kernel sits in the middle: it enforces rules and protects the hardware.

One important point: the **operating system and the kernel are not the same thing**. The kernel is the core of the OS, and the OS also includes utilities, a shell, libraries, and apps along with the kernel. Linux is a kernel, while Ubuntu is a full operating system built on the Linux kernel. Interviews often ask about this difference, so remember it.`
