          `**What if everything sits in the kernel (monolithic)?** Linux is like this — the file system, drivers, and networking all sit inside the kernel. Benefit: fast, because moving from one service to another needs only a function call. Drawback: one bad driver can bring down the whole system. The code below shows it: for 1000 system calls, the monolithic design takes only 100,000 ns.

**What if only the essentials sit in the kernel (microkernel)?** Minix and QNX are like this — the kernel holds only basic IPC, memory, and scheduling, while everything else (drivers, file system) runs in user mode. Benefit: stable and secure — a driver crash does not bring down the system. Drawback: slow, because every service must send a message to talk to another service. The same 1000 system calls take 2,000,000 ns in a microkernel — 20 times more. This is the classic monolithic vs microkernel trade-off: speed vs safety.

**What if there is a middle path (hybrid)?** Windows and macOS are hybrid — essential services in the kernel, the rest in user mode. Most systems today are hybrid, because they need neither pure speed nor pure safety, but a balance of both. When an interview asks "monolithic vs microkernel", the answer must include the pros and cons of both and this trade-off.`
