          `**Process** matlab ek program jo chal raha hai — program disk par pada hua code hai, process wohi code hai jab woh memory mein load hokar CPU par chal raha hai. Ek process apni zindagi mein kai states se guzarta hai. **New** state mein process abhi-abhi bana hai aur OS use memory de raha hai. **Ready** state mein process CPU milne ka intezar kar raha hai — woh ready queue mein khada hai. **Running** state mein process CPU par chal raha hai. **Waiting** (ya Blocked) state mein process kisi event ka intezar kar raha hai, jaise disk se data aana. **Terminated** state mein process khatam ho gaya hai aur uske resources wapas liye ja rahe hain.

States ke beech mein transitions hote hain, aur har transition ka ek reason hota hai. New se Ready: scheduler ne process ko admit kar liya. Ready se Running: scheduler ne CPU allot kar diya (ise **dispatch** kehte hain). Running se Waiting: process ne I/O maanga, jaise file read. Waiting se Ready: I/O poora ho gaya. Running se Ready: time quantum khatam ho gaya ya zyada priority wala process aa gaya (ise **preemption** kehte hain). Running se Terminated: process ne \`exit()\` call kar diya.

Ek baat yaad rakho: ek CPU par ek time par sirf ek process **running** ho sakta hai, par kai processes **ready** ya **waiting** ho sakte hain. Isi liye OS ko har process ki state track karni padti hai, aur yahi kaam PCB karta hai.`,
          `A **process** is a program that is running — a program is code sitting on the disk, while a process is that same code loaded into memory and running on the CPU. A process passes through several states in its life. In the **New** state, the process has just been created and the OS is giving it memory. In the **Ready** state, the process waits for the CPU — it stands in the ready queue. In the **Running** state, the process runs on the CPU. In the **Waiting** (or Blocked) state, the process waits for some event, like data arriving from disk. In the **Terminated** state, the process has finished and its resources are being taken back.

There are transitions between states, and every transition has a reason. New to Ready: the scheduler admitted the process. Ready to Running: the scheduler allotted the CPU (this is called **dispatch**). Running to Waiting: the process asked for I/O, like a file read. Waiting to Ready: the I/O finished. Running to Ready: the time quantum ended or a higher-priority process arrived (this is called **preemption**). Running to Terminated: the process called \`exit()\`.

Remember one thing: on one CPU, only one process can be **running** at a time, but many processes can be **ready** or **waiting**. That is why the OS must track the state of every process, and that is the job of the PCB.`
