          `**Galti 1: Program aur process ko ek samajhna.** Program disk par pada hua static code hai — woh kuch nahi karta. Process wohi program hai jab woh memory mein load hokar chal raha hai, apne PCB, registers aur memory ke saath. Ek hi program (jaise Chrome) ke kai processes ek saath chal sakte hain, har ek ka apna PCB aur apna PID hota hai. Interview mein "program vs process" ka yahi jawab hai.

**Galti 2: Waiting aur Ready ko ek samajhna.** Dono mein process CPU par nahi chal raha, par farak bahut bada hai: Ready process CPU milte hi chal padega, Waiting process pehle apne event (jaise I/O poora hona) ka intezar karega aur uske baad Ready mein jaayega. Scheduler Ready queue se process chunta hai, Waiting queue se nahi. Agar aap Waiting process ko Ready samajh kar CPU de do, toh woh turant phir ruk jaayega kyunki uska data abhi aaya hi nahi.

**Galti 3: Context switch ko free samajhna.** Context switch mein CPU ko purane process ke registers aur PC ko uske PCB mein save karna padta hai, phir naye process ka PCB load karna padta hai. Is beech CPU koi useful kaam nahi karta — yeh pure overhead hai. Neeche wala code batata hai: 100 switches, har ek 5 microsecond, total 500 microsecond. Quantum 10 ms ho toh har quantum par overhead sirf 0.05% hai, par agar quantum bahut chhota ho toh overhead badh jaata hai.`,
          `**Mistake 1: Treating a program and a process as one thing.** A program is static code sitting on the disk — it does nothing. A process is that same program loaded into memory and running, with its own PCB, registers, and memory. One program (like Chrome) can have many processes running at once, each with its own PCB and PID. That is the interview answer to "program vs process".

**Mistake 2: Treating Waiting and Ready as one thing.** In both, the process is not running on the CPU, but the difference is big: a Ready process will start running the moment it gets the CPU, while a Waiting process must first wait for its event (like I/O finishing) and only then move to Ready. The scheduler picks a process from the Ready queue, not from the Waiting queue. If you give the CPU to a Waiting process by mistake, it will stop again at once because its data has not arrived yet.

**Mistake 3: Treating a context switch as free.** In a context switch, the CPU must save the old process's registers and PC into its PCB, and then load the new process's PCB. During this time the CPU does no useful work — it is pure overhead. The code below shows it: 100 switches at 5 microseconds each give 500 microseconds in total. With a quantum of 10 ms, the overhead per quantum is only 0.05%, but if the quantum becomes very small, the overhead grows.`
