          `**Galti 1: API aur system call ko ek samajhna.** \`printf\` ek API (library function) hai, \`write\` ek system call hai. API user mode mein chalta hai aur andar se ek ya zyada system calls kar sakta hai. \`printf\` pehle text ko buffer mein rakhta hai aur jab buffer bhar jaaye, tab \`write\` system call karta hai. Isi liye 100 baar \`printf\` karne par 100 system calls nahi hote — buffering ki wajah se sirf kuch hi hote hain. Interview mein "API vs system call" ka yahi jawab hai: API library ka interface hai, system call kernel ka gate hai.

**Galti 2: fork ke return value ko galat padhna.** \`fork()\` ek baar call hota hai, par do baar return karta hai — parent mein child ka PID aur child mein 0. Beginners aksar bhool jaate hain ki fork ke baad dono processes ek hi code ko aage chalate hain, isliye \`if (pid == 0)\` se child ka code aur \`else\` se parent ka code alag karna padta hai. Agar yeh check na karo, toh parent aur child dono wahi kaam dobara karenge — jaise dono file mein likhne lagenge.

**Galti 3: wait() bhool jaana.** Agar parent child ka \`wait()\` na kare, toh child khatam hone ke baad zombie ban jaata hai. Aur agar parent child se pehle khatam ho jaaye, toh child orphan ban jaata hai. Dono hi cases mein process table ganda hota hai. Isi liye har fork ke saath wait ka plan hona chahiye.`,
          `**Mistake 1: Treating an API and a system call as one thing.** \`printf\` is an API (a library function); \`write\` is a system call. An API runs in user mode and can make one or more system calls inside. \`printf\` first keeps text in a buffer, and only when the buffer fills does it make a \`write\` system call. That is why calling \`printf\` 100 times does not cause 100 system calls — because of buffering, there are only a few. That is the interview answer to "API vs system call": an API is the interface of a library, and a system call is the gate to the kernel.

**Mistake 2: Misreading the return value of fork.** \`fork()\` is called once, but it returns twice — the child's PID in the parent, and 0 in the child. Beginners often forget that after fork, both processes run the same code ahead, so you must separate the child's code with \`if (pid == 0)\` and the parent's code with \`else\`. If you skip this check, the parent and the child will both repeat the same work — for example, both will start writing to the file.

**Mistake 3: Forgetting wait().** If the parent never calls \`wait()\` for the child, the child becomes a zombie after finishing. And if the parent finishes before the child, the child becomes an orphan. In both cases the process table gets messy. That is why every fork should come with a plan for wait.`
