          `Basic schedulers (FCFS, SJF, Round Robin) ek hi queue use karte hain, par real systems mein processes ek jaise nahi hote — kuch interactive hote hain (jaise browser), kuch batch hote hain (jaise backup), kuch real-time hote hain (jaise pacemaker). Isi liye advanced schedulers bane: **MLQ (Multilevel Queue)** processes ko alag-alag queues mein baantta hai, **MLFQ (Multilevel Feedback Queue)** processes ko unke behaviour ke hisaab se queues ke beech ghumata hai, **Lottery scheduling** tickets se CPU baantta hai, aur **real-time scheduling (RM/EDF)** deadlines ko poora karta hai.

**MLQ** mein har queue ka apna scheduler hota hai — jaise system processes ki queue, interactive ki queue, batch ki queue. Har queue ki priority alag hoti hai, aur upar wali queue khaali hone par hi neeche wali chalti hai. Par MLQ mein process ek baar jis queue mein gaya, wahi rehta hai — woh badal nahi sakta.

**MLFQ** MLQ ka smart version hai: process queues ke beech ghum sakta hai. Jo process apna quantum poora use kar le (matlab CPU-hungry hai), use neeche wali queue mein bhej diya jaata hai. Jo process jaldi I/O karke chhod de (matlab interactive hai), use upar rehne diya jaata hai. Isi feedback ki wajah se MLFQ ko process ka nature pehle se jaanne ki zaroorat nahi padti — woh khud seekh jaata hai.`,
          `Basic schedulers (FCFS, SJF, Round Robin) use one queue, but in real systems processes are not all alike — some are interactive (like a browser), some are batch (like a backup), and some are real-time (like a pacemaker). That is why advanced schedulers were made: **MLQ (Multilevel Queue)** splits processes into separate queues, **MLFQ (Multilevel Feedback Queue)** moves processes between queues based on their behaviour, **Lottery scheduling** shares the CPU with tickets, and **real-time scheduling (RM/EDF)** meets deadlines.

In **MLQ**, every queue has its own scheduler — for example, a queue for system processes, one for interactive, one for batch. Each queue has a different priority, and a lower queue runs only when the upper queue is empty. But in MLQ, once a process enters a queue, it stays there — it cannot change.

**MLFQ** is the smart version of MLQ: a process can move between queues. A process that uses up its full quantum (meaning it is CPU-hungry) is sent to a lower queue. A process that gives up the CPU quickly for I/O (meaning it is interactive) is allowed to stay up. Because of this feedback, MLFQ does not need to know a process's nature in advance — it learns it by itself.`
