          `**Edge case 1: Deadline miss.** In a real-time system, every task has a deadline — for example, a pacemaker must give a heartbeat every 1 second. If the task does not finish before its deadline, the system fails — even if the work finishes later. In **hard real-time**, a missed deadline means system failure (pacemaker, flight control); in **soft real-time**, a missed deadline means poor quality (a missed frame in a video call). That is why real-time schedulers are checked with a schedulability test in advance.

**Edge case 2: Priority inversion.** Suppose a low-priority process holds a lock, and a high-priority process waits for that same lock. In the middle, a medium-priority process arrives and takes the CPU — now the high-priority process waits for both the low and the medium one. This is called priority inversion. The fix is **priority inheritance**: give the low-priority process a temporarily high priority, so it releases the lock quickly.

**Edge case 3: MLFQ boost.** If new interactive processes keep arriving, the processes in the lower queues never run. That is why MLFQ sends all processes back to the top queue every so often (like every 100 ms) — this is called a **boost**. Without a boost, MLFQ suffers starvation.`
