          `Maan lo do processes ek hi bank account ka balance update kar rahe hain. Balance 10 hai, aur dono processes usmein 5-5 add karna chahte hain. Sahi answer 20 hona chahiye. Par agar dono processes ek saath chalein, toh dono pehle balance ko 10 padhte hain, dono apne hisaab se 15 bana lete hain, aur dono 15 wapas likh dete hain. Final balance 15 banta hai, 20 nahi — 5 rupaye ka **lost update** ho gaya. Yahi **race condition** hai: jab do ya zyada processes shared data ko ek saath access karte hain aur final result is baat par depend kare ki kaun pehle chala, toh result galat ho sakta hai.

Code ka woh hissa jahan process shared data ko read ya write karta hai, use **critical section** kehte hain. Critical section mein ek time par sirf ek hi process hona chahiye — is rule ko **mutual exclusion** kehte hain. Agar do processes ek saath critical section mein ghus jaayein, toh race condition pakki hai. Synchronization ka matlab hai processes ke beech coordination karna, taaki critical section mein hamesha ek hi process ho aur shared data hamesha sahi rahe.

Synchronization ke liye humein ek aise mechanism ki zaroorat hai jo teen shartein poori kare: **mutual exclusion** (ek time par ek hi process critical section mein), **progress** (agar koi process critical section mein nahi hai aur koi process andar jaana chahta hai, toh faisla bina der ke ho), aur **bounded waiting** (koi process hamesha ke liye wait na kare). Aage ke sections mein hum Peterson ka solution dekhenge jo in teeno sharton ko poora karta hai, aur phir semaphores aur monitors jaise advanced tools.`,
          `Suppose two processes are updating the same bank account balance. The balance is 10, and both processes want to add 5 each. The correct answer should be 20. But if both processes run at the same time, both first read the balance as 10, both compute 15 on their own, and both write 15 back. The final balance becomes 15, not 20 — an update of 5 is lost. This is a **race condition**: when two or more processes access shared data at the same time and the final result depends on which one ran first, the result can be wrong.

The part of the code where a process reads or writes shared data is called the **critical section**. Only one process should be inside the critical section at a time — this rule is called **mutual exclusion**. If two processes enter the critical section together, a race condition is certain. Synchronization means coordinating processes so that only one process is in the critical section at a time and the shared data always stays correct.

A synchronization mechanism must satisfy three conditions: **mutual exclusion** (only one process in the critical section at a time), **progress** (if no process is in the critical section and some process wants to enter, the decision must be made without delay), and **bounded waiting** (no process should wait forever). In the next sections we will see Peterson solution, which satisfies all three conditions, and later semaphores and monitors as more advanced tools.`
