          `Semaphore mein sabse common galtiyan teen hain.

**Galti 1: Wait aur signal ka order ulta karna.** Producer mein \`wait(mutex)\` ko \`wait(empty)\` se pehle likh dena deadlock deta hai. Producer mutex le kar empty par wait karega, aur consumer mutex ke bina buffer se item nikaal hi nahi sakta — dono hamesha ke liye atak jaayenge. Hamesha pehle condition wala semaphore (\`empty\` ya \`full\`), phir \`mutex\`. Signal ka order ulta ho sakta hai, par wait ka order nahi.

**Galti 2: Signal karna bhool jaana.** Agar producer item daal kar \`signal(full)\` karna bhool jaaye, toh consumer ko kabhi pata hi nahi chalega ki buffer mein item aa gaya — woh hamesha wait karta rahega, jabki buffer bhara hua hai. Har wait ka matching signal hona chahiye, jaise har lock ka unlock. Signal bhoolna deadlock jaisa hi hota hai, par use dhoondhna aur mushkil hota hai kyunki program crash nahi karta, bas ruk jaata hai.

**Galti 3: Mutex ki jagah counting semaphore use karna ya ulta.** Mutual exclusion ke liye binary semaphore chahiye. Agar aap mutex ki jagah counting semaphore (value 3) use kar do, toh teen processes ek saath critical section mein ghus jaayenge aur race condition wapas aa jaayegi. Aur agar resource counting ke liye binary semaphore use kar do, toh sirf ek hi printer use ho paayega jabki 3 khaali hain — performance waste ho jaayegi. Pehle poochho: "kya ye lock hai ya counting hai?" — jawab se semaphore ka type decide hota hai.`,
          `There are three very common semaphore mistakes.

**Mistake 1: Reversing the wait and signal order.** Writing \`wait(mutex)\` before \`wait(empty)\` in the producer causes a deadlock. The producer holds the mutex while waiting on empty, and the consumer cannot remove an item from the buffer without the mutex — both get stuck forever. Always wait on the condition semaphore (\`empty\` or \`full\`) first, then on \`mutex\`. The signal order can vary, but the wait order cannot.

**Mistake 2: Forgetting a signal.** If the producer puts an item in and forgets \`signal(full)\`, the consumer never learns that an item arrived — it waits forever while the buffer sits full. Every wait needs a matching signal, just as every lock needs an unlock. A forgotten signal behaves like a deadlock, but it is harder to find because the program does not crash; it simply stops.

**Mistake 3: Using a counting semaphore where a mutex belongs, or the reverse.** Mutual exclusion needs a binary semaphore. If you use a counting semaphore (value 3) as a mutex, three processes enter the critical section together and the race condition returns. And if you use a binary semaphore for resource counting, only one printer gets used while 3 sit idle — performance is wasted. First ask: "is this a lock or a count?" — the answer decides the semaphore type.`
