          `Monitor mein bhi galtiyan hoti hain, par semaphore se kam.

**Galti 1: Signal karna bhool jaana.** Agar consumer item nikaal kar \`notFull\` par signal karna bhool jaaye, toh waiting producer hamesha soya rahega, jabki buffer mein jagah khaali hai. Monitor mein mutual exclusion automatic hai, par condition ka signal programmer ko hi dena hota hai. Rule yaad rakho: jab bhi condition badle (buffer mein jagah bani ya item aaya), signal do.

**Galti 2: Galat condition par wait karna.** Producer ko \`notFull\` par wait karna hai, \`notEmpty\` par nahi. Agar producer galat condition par wait kare, toh use kabhi signal hi nahi milega, kyunki consumer sirf \`notFull\` ko signal karta hai. Har wait ka matching signal hona chahiye — condition ka naam hi batata hai ki kaun kisko jagata hai.

**Galti 3: Nested monitor call karna.** Agar ek monitor ke andar se doosre monitor ka function call kiya jaaye aur doosra monitor busy ho, toh pehla monitor bhi block ho jaata hai aur deadlock ho sakta hai. Isi liye monitor ko chhota aur simple rakha jaata hai — ek monitor mein sirf wahi data rakho jo usi se related ho. Java mein \`synchronized\` methods aur \`wait()\`/\`notify()\` monitor ka hi implementation hain, aur wahan bhi nested synchronized calls se deadlock hota hai.`,
          `Monitors can also be misused, though less often than semaphores.

**Mistake 1: Forgetting a signal.** If the consumer removes an item but forgets to signal \`notFull\`, the waiting producer sleeps forever even though space is free in the buffer. Mutual exclusion is automatic in a monitor, but the condition signal must still come from the programmer. Remember the rule: whenever a condition changes (space appears in the buffer or an item arrives), send a signal.

**Mistake 2: Waiting on the wrong condition.** The producer must wait on \`notFull\`, not on \`notEmpty\`. If the producer waits on the wrong condition, it never gets a signal, because the consumer only signals \`notFull\`. Every wait needs a matching signal — the condition name itself tells you who wakes whom.

**Mistake 3: Calling a nested monitor.** If a function inside one monitor calls a function of another monitor and the second monitor is busy, the first monitor blocks too and a deadlock can happen. That is why monitors are kept small and simple — a monitor should hold only the data related to it. In Java, \`synchronized\` methods with \`wait()\` and \`notify()\` are an implementation of monitors, and nested synchronized calls cause deadlocks there as well.`
