          `Edge cases woh situations hain jo normal test mein nahi aati, par interview aur real bugs mein yahi phansate hain. **1. Signal jab koi wait na kar raha ho.** Monitor mein signal ka koi effect nahi hota agar koi process us condition par wait hi nahi kar raha — signal kho jaata hai. Semaphore mein signal counter badha deta hai, par monitor mein aisa nahi hota. Isi liye condition ko hamesha check karna chahiye: "agar buffer khaali hai, tabhi wait karo" — bina check ke wait karna galat hai.

**2. Spurious wakeup.** Kabhi-kabhi waiting process bina signal ke bhi jaag jaata hai — ise spurious wakeup kehte hain. Isi liye condition ko \`if\` se nahi, \`while\` loop se check karna chahiye: jaagne ke baad phir se check karo ki condition sach mein poori hui ya nahi. Java mein bhi \`wait()\` ko hamesha while loop mein likha jaata hai, yahi wajah hai.

**3. Multiple waiters.** Agar 3 producers \`notFull\` par wait kar rahe hain aur ek signal aata hai, toh sirf ek hi jaagta hai — kaunsa, ye FIFO order par depend karta hai. Baaki do sote rehte hain. Agar sabko jagana ho, toh \`signalAll\` (Java mein \`notifyAll\`) use karna padta hai. Interview mein signal vs signalAll ka farak aksar poochha jaata hai.

**4. Monitor ke andar lamba kaam.** Agar ek process monitor ke andar bahut der tak kaam kare, toh baaki processes entry queue mein atak jaate hain. Monitor ke procedures ko chhota rakho — lamba calculation monitor ke bahar karo, sirf shared data ka update andar karo.`,
          `Edge cases are the situations that never appear in normal tests but cause real bugs and interview traps. **1. A signal with no waiter.** In a monitor, a signal has no effect if no process is waiting on that condition — the signal is lost. In a semaphore, a signal increases a counter, but a monitor does not work that way. That is why the condition must always be checked first: "wait only if the buffer is empty" — waiting without a check is wrong.

**2. Spurious wakeup.** Sometimes a waiting process wakes up even without a signal — this is called a spurious wakeup. That is why a condition must be checked with a \`while\` loop, not an \`if\`: after waking up, check again whether the condition really holds. In Java too, \`wait()\` is always written inside a while loop, for exactly this reason.

**3. Multiple waiters.** If 3 producers wait on \`notFull\` and one signal arrives, only one of them wakes — which one depends on FIFO order. The other two keep sleeping. To wake all of them, \`signalAll\` (in Java, \`notifyAll\`) must be used. The difference between signal and signalAll is often asked in interviews.

**4. Long work inside a monitor.** If a process works inside a monitor for a long time, the other processes get stuck in the entry queue. Keep monitor procedures short — do long calculations outside the monitor and only update shared data inside it.`
