          `**Banker ka algorithm** deadlock se bachne ka sabse famous algorithm hai. Ise Dijkstra ne banaya tha. Idea bank se aaya hai: bank ke paas limited cash hai aur customers ne loan ke liye maximum amount pehle se bata rakhi hai. Bank har request par ye check karta hai ki agar ye loan de diya, toh kya baaki saare customers ko bhi unka loan de kar kaam poora karwaya ja sakta hai? Agar haan, toh state **safe** hai aur loan diya ja sakta hai. Agar nahi, toh request ko wait karwana padta hai.

Operating system mein bank cash ki jagah resources hote hain (printer, memory, CPU) aur customers ki jagah processes hote hain. Har process pehle se batata hai ki use maximum kitne resources chahiye — ye **Max** matrix hai. Process ko ab tak jo resources mil chuke hain, wo **Allocation** matrix hai. **Need = Max - Allocation** — matlab process ko abhi aur kitne resources chahiye. **Available** batata hai ki system mein abhi kitne resources free hain.

**Safe state** ka matlab hai ki processes ko kisi order mein chala kar sabko unki need poori ki ja sakti hai — is order ko **safe sequence** kehte hain. **Unsafe state** ka matlab hai ki koi safe sequence nahi hai; unsafe ka matlab ye nahi ki deadlock pakka hai, par deadlock ho sakta hai, isliye OS unsafe state mein jaane se mana kar deta hai. **Deadlock state** ka matlab hai ki processes ek doosre ka wait kar rahe hain aur koi bhi aage nahi badh sakta.`,
          `**Banker algorithm** is the most famous algorithm for avoiding deadlock. Dijkstra created it. The idea comes from a bank: the bank has limited cash, and customers have already declared the maximum loan they may need. On every request the bank checks whether, after granting this loan, all remaining customers can still be given their loans and finish their work. If yes, the state is **safe** and the loan can be granted. If no, the request must wait.

In an operating system, resources (printers, memory, CPU) replace the bank cash, and processes replace the customers. Each process declares in advance the maximum resources it needs — this is the **Max** matrix. The resources a process has already received form the **Allocation** matrix. **Need = Max - Allocation** — how many more resources the process still needs. **Available** records how many resources are currently free in the system.

A **safe state** means the processes can be run in some order so that every need is fulfilled — this order is called the **safe sequence**. An **unsafe state** means no safe sequence exists; unsafe does not guarantee a deadlock, but a deadlock becomes possible, so the OS refuses to enter an unsafe state. A **deadlock state** means the processes wait on each other and none of them can move forward.`
