          `Edge cases woh situations hain jo normal test mein nahi aati, par interview aur real bugs mein yahi phansate hain. **1. Belady ka anomaly.** FIFO mein frames badhane par faults kam hone chahiye, par kabhi-kabhi faults badh jaate hain! Reference string [1, 2, 3, 4, 1, 2, 5, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5] par FIFO 3 frames mein 9 faults deta hai aur 4 frames mein 10 faults deta hai. Zyada memory, zyada faults — yahi Belady ka anomaly hai. LRU aur Optimal mein ye anomaly nahi hoti, kyunki woh stack algorithms hain. Interview mein ye example pakka poochha jaata hai — numbers yaad rakho: 3 frames = 9, 4 frames = 10.

**2. Thrashing.** Agar process ko kaam karne ke liye jitne pages chahiye (working set), usse kam frames milein, toh process baar-baar page fault karega — page laao, doosra page nikalo, phir wahi page dobara laao. CPU ka zyada time page faults handle karne mein chala jaata hai aur asli kaam ruk jaata hai. Isi ko **thrashing** kehte hain. Solution hai process ko aur frames dena ya ek process ko suspend karke uske frames doosre ko dena.

**3. Reference string mein sirf ek hi page ho.** Agar process sirf page 5 ko baar-baar maangta hai, toh pehle access par 1 fault hoga aur baaki sab hit honge — algorithm se koi farak nahi padta. Aise edge case mein fault count frames par depend nahi karta.

**4. Frames pages se zyada hon.** Agar frames ki sankhya alag-alag pages se zyada hai, toh ek bhi replacement nahi hoga — har page ek baar fault karega aur phir hamesha memory mein rahega.`,
          `Edge cases are the situations that never appear in normal tests but cause real bugs and interview traps. **1. Belady anomaly.** In FIFO, adding frames should reduce faults, but sometimes faults increase! On the reference string [1, 2, 3, 4, 1, 2, 5, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5], FIFO gives 9 faults with 3 frames and 10 faults with 4 frames. More memory, more faults — this is Belady anomaly. LRU and Optimal do not have this anomaly, because they are stack algorithms. This example is certainly asked in interviews — remember the numbers: 3 frames = 9, 4 frames = 10.

**2. Thrashing.** If a process gets fewer frames than the pages it needs to work (its working set), it faults again and again — bring a page in, evict another page, then bring the same page in again. The CPU spends most of its time handling page faults and real work stops. This is called **thrashing**. The fix is to give the process more frames, or to suspend one process and give its frames to another.

**3. The reference string has only one page.** If a process asks for page 5 again and again, there is 1 fault on the first access and hits after that — the algorithm makes no difference. In this edge case, the fault count does not depend on frames.

**4. Frames outnumber the distinct pages.** If the number of frames is larger than the number of distinct pages, no replacement ever happens — every page faults once and then stays in memory forever.`
