          `Ab hum "what-if" sawaal poochhte hain — yahi woh sawaal hain jo interviewer poochhta hai.

**What if: Optimal algorithm use karein?** Optimal woh page nikaalta hai jo future mein sabse der se use hoga. Ye sabse kam faults deta hai, par iske liye future pata hona chahiye, jo real system mein possible nahi hai. Isliye Optimal sirf benchmark hai — FIFO aur LRU ko isse compare karke batate hain ki kaunsa behtar hai. Exam mein Optimal ka dry run aksar aata hai, isliye uska tareeka seekh lo: aage dekho, jo page sabse door use hoga use nikaalo.

**What if: LRU ko saste mein implement karna ho?** Pure LRU mein har access par list update karni padti hai, jo costly hai. Isliye real systems **Clock algorithm** (second chance) use karte hain: pages ko circle mein rakho, har page ke saath ek reference bit hoti hai. Clock ka hand ghoomta hai — agar page ka reference bit 1 hai, toh use 0 karke second chance do; agar 0 hai, toh use nikaal do. Clock LRU ka approximate version hai aur bahut sasta hai.

**What if: thrashing se bachna ho?** **Working set model** kehta hai ki har process ko uske working set jitne frames milne chahiye — working set matlab pichle kuch accesses mein use hue alag pages. Agar saare processes ke working sets ka total available frames se zyada ho, toh ek process ko suspend kar do. Yahi thrashing se bachne ka sahi tareeka hai. Neeche wala code Belady ke anomaly ko dikhata hai — 3 frames par 9 faults, 4 frames par 10 faults.`,
          `Now let us ask "what-if" questions — these are exactly the questions interviewers ask.

**What if we use the Optimal algorithm?** Optimal evicts the page that will be used latest in the future. It gives the fewest faults, but it needs the future to be known, which is impossible in a real system. So Optimal is only a benchmark — FIFO and LRU are compared against it to show which is better. The Optimal dry run often appears in exams, so learn its method: look ahead, and evict the page that will be used furthest away.

**What if LRU must be implemented cheaply?** Pure LRU updates a list on every access, which is costly. Real systems therefore use the **Clock algorithm** (second chance): pages sit in a circle, each with a reference bit. The clock hand moves around — if a page reference bit is 1, set it to 0 and give it a second chance; if it is 0, evict it. Clock is an approximate version of LRU and is very cheap.

**What if thrashing must be avoided?** The **working set model** says every process should get as many frames as its working set — the working set means the distinct pages used in the recent accesses. If the total of all working sets exceeds the available frames, suspend one process. This is the correct way to avoid thrashing. The code below shows Belady anomaly — 9 faults with 3 frames, 10 faults with 4 frames.`
