          `**Virtual memory** ek aisi technique hai jismein process ko lagta hai ki uske paas bahut badi memory hai, jabki asli physical memory (RAM) chhoti hoti hai. Process ka **logical address space** (virtual memory) RAM se bada ho sakta hai — jaise 4 GB RAM par 16 GB ka process chal sakta hai. Ye kaise hota hai? Kyunki process ke saare pages ek saath RAM mein nahi hote. Jo pages abhi use ho rahe hain, woh RAM mein hote hain; baaki pages disk par (swap space mein) hote hain.

**Demand paging** ka matlab hai: page ko RAM mein tabhi laao jab process use maange. Program shuru hote time koi bhi page RAM mein nahi hota — saare pages ke valid bit 0 hote hain. Jab process pehle page ko access karta hai, toh **page fault** hota hai, OS page ko disk se la kar RAM mein daalta hai aur valid bit ko 1 kar deta hai. Isse program fast start hota hai aur memory bhi bachti hai, kyunki program ke woh hisse jo kabhi chalte hi nahi (jaise error handling code), woh disk par hi rehte hain.

Virtual memory ke teen bade fayde hain: pehla, process RAM se bada ho sakta hai; doosra, ek saath zyada processes RAM mein chal sakte hain (kyunki har process ka sirf ek hissa RAM mein hota hai); teesra, process ko doosre process ki memory se protection milti hai, kyunki har process ka apna page table hota hai.`,
          `**Virtual memory** is a technique in which a process feels like it has a very large memory, while the real physical memory (RAM) is smaller. A process **logical address space** (virtual memory) can be larger than RAM — for example, a 16 GB process can run on 4 GB of RAM. How does this work? Because not all of a process pages sit in RAM at once. The pages in use right now sit in RAM; the rest sit on disk (in swap space).

**Demand paging** means: bring a page into RAM only when the process asks for it. When a program starts, no page is in RAM — every page has a valid bit of 0. When the process accesses the first page, a **page fault** happens, the OS brings the page from disk into RAM and sets the valid bit to 1. This makes programs start fast and saves memory, because the parts of a program that never run (such as error handling code) stay on disk.

Virtual memory has three big advantages: first, a process can be larger than RAM; second, more processes can run in RAM at once (because only part of each process sits in RAM); third, a process is protected from another process memory, because every process has its own page table.`
