          `**What if journaling na ho?** Bina journaling ke, agar light chali jaaye toh file system ko pata nahi chalta kaunsa kaam adhoora reh gaya. File likhi gayi par uska inode update nahi hua, ya directory update hui par blocks allocate nahi hue — aise aadhe kaam se file system corrupt ho jaata hai. Boot par poori disk scan karni padti hai, jo ghanton lag sakta hai. Journaling mein har badlaav pehle ek journal (diary) mein likha jaata hai, phir asli jagah par. Crash ke baad OS journal padh kar adhoore kaam ko ya toh poora karta hai ya wapas le leta hai. Isi liye journaling wale file systems fast recover hote hain.

**What if directory hi kho jaaye?** Data disk par rehta hai par naam se nahi milta. Recovery tools phir disk ko block-by-block scan karke file ke type (jaise photo ka header) se files dhoondhte hain. Yeh slow hai aur naam wapas nahi milte — files ko \`file001.jpg\` jaise naam milte hain. Isi liye backup sirf data ka nahi, directory structure ka bhi hona chahiye.

**What if do files ka naam ek jaisa ho?** Ek directory mein do files ka naam ek jaisa nahi ho sakta, kyunki directory ek naam ko ek hi inode se jodti hai. Doosri file banane par OS error deta hai ya purani file ko overwrite karta hai, system par depend karta hai. Alag folders mein ek hi naam chal sakta hai, kyunki har folder ki apni directory hoti hai.`,
          `**What if there were no journaling?** Without journaling, a power cut leaves the file system with no record of which work was left incomplete. A file may be written but its inode not updated, or a directory updated but blocks not allocated — such half-finished work corrupts the file system. At boot the whole disk must be scanned, which can take hours. With journaling, every change is first written to a journal (a diary), then to its real place. After a crash the OS reads the journal and either completes the unfinished work or rolls it back. That is why journaling file systems recover fast.

**What if the directory itself is lost?** The data stays on the disk but cannot be found by name. Recovery tools then scan the disk block by block and find files from their type (such as a photo header). This is slow, and the names are not recovered — files get names like \`file001.jpg\`. That is why a backup must cover the directory structure too, not only the data.

**What if two files have the same name?** Two files in one directory cannot have the same name, because a directory connects one name to one inode. Creating a second file gives an error or overwrites the old file, depending on the system. The same name can exist in different folders, because each folder has its own directory.`
