          `**What if RM ki jagah EDF use karein?** EDF mein jis task ki deadline sabse nazdeek hai, woh pehle chalta hai. EDF RM se behtar hai kyunki woh 100% utilization tak schedulable hai — total utilization 1 tak ho toh bhi tasks chal sakte hain. RM mein bound 0.78 hai, isliye 0.8 utilization par bhi RM fail kar sakta hai jabki EDF chal sakta hai. Par EDF ka kharcha zyada hai — har baar deadline check karni padti hai aur priority badalti rehti hai. Neeche wale code mein EDF order dikhaya gaya hai: deadline 4 wala B pehle, phir 7 wala C, phir 10 wala A.

**What if priority ulti ho jaaye?** **Priority inversion** tab hota hai jab kam priority wala task zyada priority wale task ko rok de. Jaise kam priority wala task ek lock pakad kar baitha hai, aur zyada priority wala task usi lock ka wait kar raha hai — beech mein beech wali priority ke tasks chalte rehte hain aur kam priority wala task lock chhod hi nahi pata. Isse bachne ke liye **priority inheritance** use hota hai — lock pakadne wale task ki priority tab tak badha di jaati hai jab tak woh lock na chhode.

**What if hard real-time mein ek deadline miss ho jaaye?** Hard real-time mein ek miss bhi fail hai. Isliye hard real-time systems mein pehle se test kiya jaata hai ki saari deadlines poori hongi — utilization bound aur schedulability test se. Agar test fail ho, toh tasks ko badla jaata hai ya hardware fast kiya jaata hai, system ko chalaya nahi jaata.`,
          `**What if EDF is used instead of RM?** In EDF, the task with the nearest deadline runs first. EDF is better than RM because it is schedulable up to 100% utilization — tasks can run even when total utilization is 1. In RM, the bound is 0.78, so RM can fail at 0.8 utilization while EDF can still run. But EDF costs more — the deadline must be checked every time, and priority keeps changing. The code below shows the EDF order: B with deadline 4 first, then C with 7, then A with 10.

**What if priority turns upside down?** **Priority inversion** happens when a low-priority task blocks a high-priority task. For example, a low-priority task holds a lock, and a high-priority task waits for that same lock — meanwhile, middle-priority tasks keep running, and the low-priority task never gets to release the lock. To avoid this, **priority inheritance** is used — the priority of the task holding the lock is raised until it releases the lock.

**What if one deadline is missed in hard real-time?** In hard real-time, even one miss is failure. That is why hard real-time systems are tested in advance to make sure all deadlines will be met — with the utilization bound and a schedulability test. If the test fails, the tasks are changed or the hardware is made faster; the system is not run as it is.`
