          `Sabse bada example hai **cloud computing**. Amazon, Google aur Microsoft ke data centres mein lakhon physical servers hain, par un par crore VMs chalti hain. Jab aap cloud par ek server rent karte ho, toh aapko ek poori physical machine nahi milti — aapko ek VM milti hai jo ek bade server ka hissa hai. Hypervisor yeh baantne ka kaam karta hai, aur jab aapka kaam khatam ho jaaye toh VM delete karke RAM wapas le li jaati hai.

Doosra example aapke apne laptop ka hai. **VirtualBox** ya **VMware** se aap Windows laptop par Linux VM chala sakte ho, bina Windows delete kiye. Students isi tareeke se ek hi laptop par do OS seekhte hain, aur companies purane software ko purane OS wali VM mein chalati hain.

Teesra example hai **Docker containers**. Ek developer apna web app ek container mein pack karta hai — code, libraries, settings, sab kuch. Phir wahi container uske laptop par, test server par aur production server par bilkul ek jaisa chalta hai. Isi ko kehte hain "mere laptop par toh chal raha tha" problem ka end: container ke andar environment hamesha ek jaisa rehta hai.`,
          `The biggest example is **cloud computing**. The data centres of Amazon, Google, and Microsoft hold hundreds of thousands of physical servers, but they run tens of millions of VMs. When you rent a server in the cloud, you do not get a whole physical machine — you get a VM that is one part of a big server. The hypervisor does the sharing, and when your work ends, the VM is deleted and its RAM is taken back.

A second example is your own laptop. With **VirtualBox** or **VMware** you can run a Linux VM on a Windows laptop without deleting Windows. Students use this to learn two operating systems on one laptop, and companies run old software inside a VM with an old OS.

A third example is **Docker containers**. A developer packs a web app into a container — code, libraries, settings, everything. Then the same container runs exactly the same way on her laptop, on the test server, and on the production server. This ends the "but it works on my laptop" problem: inside a container, the environment is always the same.`
