          `**1. Zombie process.** Jab ek child process khatam ho jaaye aur parent uska exit status na padhe, toh child **zombie** ban jaata hai — woh mar chuka hai, par process table mein uski entry rehti hai. Zombie CPU ya memory nahi khata, sirf table mein jagah leta hai. Parent ko \`wait\` karna chahiye taaki zombie saaf ho jaaye.

**2. Page fault.** Jab process kisi virtual page ko chhoo le jo abhi RAM mein nahi hai, toh **page fault** hota hai. OS use page ko disk se laakar RAM mein rakhta hai aur page table update karta hai. Page fault error nahi hai — yeh virtual memory ka normal hissa hai, aur har program start hote samay hazaaron page faults hote hain.

**3. Hard link.** Ek hi inode ke do naam ho sakte hain — isi ko **hard link** kehte hain. Dono naam ek hi data ko dikhate hain, aur ek naam delete karne par doosra naam aur data safe rehta hai. Data tabhi delete hota hai jab aakhri naam bhi delete ho jaaye.

**4. Process ka memory badhna.** Agar process zyada memory maange aur RAM khatam ho jaaye, toh Linux **OOM killer** chalata hai — woh sabse zyada memory khaane wale process ko maar deta hai taaki system zinda rahe. Isi liye chrome kabhi-kabhi achanak band ho jaata hai.`,
          `**1. Zombie process.** When a child process ends and its parent does not read its exit status, the child becomes a **zombie** — it is dead, but its entry stays in the process table. A zombie eats no CPU or memory, it only takes space in the table. The parent should call \`wait\` so the zombie is cleaned up.

**2. Page fault.** When a process touches a virtual page that is not in RAM yet, a **page fault** happens. The OS brings that page from the disk into RAM and updates the page table. A page fault is not an error — it is a normal part of virtual memory, and thousands of page faults happen every time a program starts.

**3. Hard link.** One inode can have two names — this is called a **hard link**. Both names point to the same data, and deleting one name leaves the other name and the data safe. The data is deleted only when the last name is also deleted.

**4. A process growing its memory.** If a process asks for more memory and the RAM runs out, Linux runs the **OOM killer** — it kills the process eating the most memory so the system stays alive. That is why Chrome sometimes closes suddenly.`
